       r, c = queue.popleft()\n            for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n                if 0 <= nr < m and 0 <= nc < n and rooms[nr][nc] == INF:\n                    rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[r][c] + 1\n                    queue.append((nr, nc))\n","approach":"**Key insight: a single BFS seeded with every gate computes, for each room, the distance to the closest gate, because BFS reaches cells in order of distance from the nearest source.**\n\n**Algorithm.** Put all gate cells in a queue. Pop `(r, c)`; for each neighbour still equal to `INF`, set it to `rooms[r][c] + 1` and enqueue it. Walls (`-1`) and already-filled rooms are never `INF`, so they are skipped automatically. No explicit level counter is needed because the distance is stored in the cell.\n\n**Why it works.** When a room is first dequeued-into, the popping cell is at the minimum distance d among all cells processed so far, so d + 1 is the room's true shortest distance. Overwriting `INF` on first touch doubles as the visited check.\n\n**Common mistakes:** BFS from each room independently (O((mn)^2)); using `<` comparison updates (Dijkstra-style) that are unnecessary for unit weights; forgetting walls; mutating `rooms` while iterating to collect the gates (collect first, then expand).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Same problem with a single gate and obstacles? (ordinary BFS.) The grid has weighted terrain? (Dijkstra with a heap.) Return, for each room, *which* gate is closest? (store the source id alongside the distance when enqueueing.) Rotting Oranges and 01 Matrix are the same template, can you point out the shared structure?","complexity":{"time":"O(m * n)","space":"O(m * n)"},"similar":["rotting-oranges","01-matrix","shortest-path-in-binary-matrix"],"number":103,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def wallsAndGates(self, rooms: List[List[int]]) -> None:\n        pass\n"}